Yes. For the release train, you can absolutely make it one source of truth.

Right now you have to keep these in sync in [flake.nix](/home/pablo/dotfiles/flake.nix):

- `nixpkgs/nixos-25.11`
- `home-manager/release-25.11`

That is annoying, and for a beginner it’s a good thing to simplify.

The important exception is:
- `home.stateVersion`
- `system.stateVersion`

Those are not “which release train am I on?”
They are compatibility settings, so they should stay separate and usually change rarely.

A clean beginner-friendly pattern is this in `flake.nix`:

```nix
{
  description = "NixOS from Scratch";

  inputs =
    let
      release = "25.11";
    in
    {
      nixpkgs.url = "nixpkgs/nixos-${release}";
      home-manager = {
        url = "github:nix-community/home-manager/release-${release}";
        inputs.nixpkgs.follows = "nixpkgs";
      };
    };

  outputs = { self, nixpkgs, home-manager, ... }: {
    nixosConfigurations.nixos-btw = nixpkgs.lib.nixosSystem {
      system = "x86_64-linux";
      modules = [
        ./configuration.nix
        home-manager.nixosModules.home-manager
        {
          home-manager = {
            useGlobalPkgs = true;
            useUserPackages = true;
            users.pablo = import ./home.nix;
            backupFileExtension = "backup";
          };
        }
      ];
    };
  };
}
```

Then when you want to move from `25.11` to `26.05`, you change only:

```nix
release = "26.05";
```

Then run:

```bash
nix flake update
nrs
```

Simple rule:
- `release = "25.11"` = which train you ride
- `home.stateVersion` and `system.stateVersion` = old compatibility markers, leave them alone unless you have a specific reason

If you want, I can make that cleanup in your `flake.nix` now.

